    peopleAndChurch: "• The Ephesian Elders: Local pastors and overseers (presbyteroi / episkopoi) who journeyed thirty miles south to receive Paul's final charge.\n\n• The Apostle Paul: Poured out his heart in his farewell charge before facing imprisonment in Jerusalem.\n\n• Luke the Evangelist: Eyewitness who recorded the emotional farewell scene in Acts 20.\n\n• Trophimus the Ephesian: Traveling companion whom Paul later had to leave sick at Miletus during his final travels (2 Timothy 4:20).",
    politicalInsights: "• Maritime Shipping Hub on the Aegean: Miletus's location thirty miles south of Ephesus allowed coastal vessels to avoid the complex estuary winds of the Cayster River, enabling Paul to control his travel schedule to reach Jerusalem by Pentecost.\n\n• Synagogue Inscription in the Theater: German excavations in the Miletus theater uncovered a 5th-century reserved seating inscription in Greek: 'Place of the Jews, who are also called the God-fearing' (topos Eioudeon ton kai theosebion), demonstrating the recognized civic standing of the Jewish diaspora.\n\n• Silting of the Maeander River: Over the centuries, the Maeander River deposited vast alluvial silt, transforming the Latmian Gulf into Lake Bafa and pushing Miletus six miles inland, leaving its grand theater marooned in the plain.\n\n• Archaeological Excavations: Discoveries by Theodor Wiegand include the monumental Roman Theater, the Delphinion (sanctuary of Apollo Delphinios), the Baths of Faustina, and the Sacred Way.",
    eraChronology: "• Spring 57 AD: Paul summons the Ephesian elders to Miletus; delivers his Farewell Address.\n• Spring 57 AD: Emotional kneeling prayer on the beach; Paul embarks for Tyre and Caesarea.\n• ~66 AD: Paul visits Miletus on his final journey; leaves Trophimus sick (2 Timothy 4:20).\n• 2nd Century AD: Miletus flourishes under Antonine emperors before river silting chokes its harbors.",
